1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,162
Chapter 65: Dermatological Disorders.

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:29,900
Pregnancy-Specific Dermatoses Other Dermatological Conditions Dermatological Treatment References Pregnancy-Specific Dermatoses Four dermatoses considered unique to pregnancy are pemphigoid gestationis (PG), pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP), atopic eruption of pregnancy (AEP), and intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy.

4
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:34,137
Descriptions of these are given in Table 65-1.

5
00:00:34,137 --> 00:00:41,050
Their gross appearance can be similar to each other or to other skin disorders, and pruritus is a common feature.

6
00:00:41,050 --> 00:00:47,712
Of these four, adverse fetal outcomes are linked only with PG and intrahepatic cholestasis.

7
00:00:47,712 --> 00:00:53,187
The latter is discussed in detail in Chapter 58 (p. 1032).

8
00:00:53,187 --> 00:01:04,850
It generally lacks primary skin lesions, but pruritus is usually associated with abnormally elevated serum bile acid levels and mildly increased hepatic transaminase levels.

9
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:07,425
Table 65-1.

10
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:12,375
Pregnancy-specific Dermatoses # Pemphigoid Gestationis.

11
00:01:12,375 --> 00:01:19,250
In this rare autoimmune bullous disease, pruritic papules and urticarial plaques initially form.

12
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:23,500
Vesicles or bullae usually follow after 1 to 2 weeks.

13
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:31,462
Lesions frequently involve umbilicus and other skin surfaces but typically spare mucous membranes, scalp, and face .

14
00:01:31,462 --> 00:01:34,050
Figure 65-1.

15
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:36,562
Pemphigoid gestationis.

16
00:01:36,562 --> 00:01:40,662
A. Abdominal plaques classically involve the umbilicus.

17
00:01:40,662 --> 00:01:44,037
B. Blistered lesions on the wrist and forearm.

18
00:01:44,037 --> 00:01:47,825
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Kara Ehlers.

19
00:01:47,825 --> 00:01:53,712
Previously termed herpes gestationis, PG is not related to the herpesvirus.

20
00:01:53,712 --> 00:02:07,962
Instead, maternal immunoglobulin G (IgG) antibodies target collagen XVII found in the basement membrane of skin and amnionic epithelium (Kelly, 1988; Sadik, 2016).

21
00:02:07,962 --> 00:02:14,125
Collagen XVII is also termed bullous pemphigoid 180 (BP180).

22
00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:23,125
Autoantibody binding to BP180 activates complement to promote eosinophil chemotaxis to the antigen-antibody complexes.

23
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:29,225
Eosinophilic degranulation damages the dermal–epidermal junction and leads to blistering.

24
00:02:29,225 --> 00:02:34,187
Pregnancy In most cases, PG develops during a first pregnancy.

25
00:02:34,187 --> 00:02:40,012
Most subsequent pregnancies also are affected, usually earlier and more severely .

26
00:02:40,012 --> 00:02:50,637
Additionally, other autoimmune diseases, especially Graves disease, are frequent in affected women (Jenkins, 1999; Shornick, 1992).

27
00:02:50,637 --> 00:02:58,212
PG usually begins during the second or third trimester, but postpartum onset or exacerbation is common .

28
00:02:58,212 --> 00:03:03,037
The disease course is often marked by antepartum flares and remissions.

29
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:17,875
In cases with early-onset and blistering, PG can be associated with preterm birth, oligohydramnios, and fetal-growth restriction (Al-Saif, 2016; Chi, 2009; Dabas, 2018).

30
00:03:17,875 --> 00:03:26,525
One theory suggests mild placental insufficiency stems from IgG and complement deposition along the amnionic basement membrane .

31
00:03:26,525 --> 00:03:32,812
Thus, surveillance for and management of these complications in affected pregnancies is reasonable.

32
00:03:32,812 --> 00:03:41,312
In 5 to 10 percent of cases, IgG antibodies passively transfer from the mother to cause similar skin lesions in the newborn .

33
00:03:41,312 --> 00:03:50,400
These eruptions in the neonate require only wound care and clear spontaneously within a few weeks as the passively acquired IgG levels drop.

34
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:57,612
After delivery, maternal lesions resolve without scarring, and most women are disease-free after 6 months .

35
00:03:57,612 --> 00:04:01,437
In some, however, resolution is protracted.

36
00:04:01,437 --> 00:04:10,500
Disease may be exacerbated during menses or by oral contraceptives (Fania, 2017; Semkova, 2009).

37
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:16,575
Diagnosis and Treatment Before bullae form, these lesions may resemble PUPPP.

38
00:04:16,575 --> 00:04:33,425
Other diagnoses include bullous pemphigoid, pustular psoriasis, erythema multiforme, linear IgA bullous dermatosis, urticaria, dermatitis herpetiformis, allergic contact dermatitis, and AEP (Lipozenčić, 2012).

39
00:04:33,425 --> 00:04:42,987
Potentially life-threatening druginduced blistering syndromes, such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, also must be excluded .

40
00:04:42,987 --> 00:04:49,537
Diagnostically, immunofluorescent staining of a skin punch biopsy sample is the most accurate.

41
00:04:49,537 --> 00:05:02,112
With this, C3 complement and sometimes IgG are seen deposited along the basement membrane between the epidermis and the dermis (Katz, 1976; Tani, 2015).

42
00:05:02,112 --> 00:05:09,825
Circulating IgG antibodies against BP180 also can be detected in maternal serum using enzyme-linked

43
00:05:09,988 --> 00:05:17,188
immunosorbent assay (ELISA) (Al Saif, 2017; Sitaru, 2004).

44
00:05:17,138 --> 00:05:20,175
Pruritus with PG can be severe.

45
00:05:20,175 --> 00:05:28,700
Early in its course, topical high-potency corticosteroids and oral antihistamines may be effective (p. 1158).

46
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:36,088
For refractory lesions, an oral corticosteroid is provided, and prednisone or prednisolone is preferable.

47
00:05:36,088 --> 00:05:43,325
Either is inactivated by placental 11-βhydroxylase enzyme, which significantly lowers fetal exposure .

48
00:05:43,325 --> 00:05:51,863
Oral prednisone, 0.5 to 1 mg/kg daily and gradually tapered to a maintenance dose, is one option.

49
00:05:51,863 --> 00:06:06,900
In rare intractable cases, intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy, cyclosporine, or plasmapheresis has been used (Huilaja, 2015; Marker, 2011; Yang, 2018).

50
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:19,775
Rituximab early in pregnancy also is described, but its later use carries the risk of transient newborn immunosuppression risks (Chakravarty, 2011; Tourte, 2017).

51
00:06:19,775 --> 00:06:24,500
# Pruritic Urticarial Papules and Plaques of Pregnancy.

52
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:35,763
This common pregnancy-specific dermatosis is characterized by intensely pruritic, 1- to 2-mm erythematous papules that coalesce to form urticarial plaques.

53
00:06:35,763 --> 00:06:42,650
Also known as polymorphic eruption of pregnancy, PUPPP usually appears late in pregnancy .

54
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:45,575
Rarely, onset is postpartum .

55
00:06:45,575 --> 00:06:50,850
The rash affects the abdomen and proximal thighs in 97 percent of women .

56
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:56,225
Lesions often initially form within striae but spare periumbilical skin.

57
00:06:56,225 --> 00:07:00,225
The face, palms, and soles are rarely involved .

58
00:07:00,225 --> 00:07:07,375
It is more frequent in whites and nulliparas, those with multifetal gestation, and those carrying a male fetus .

59
00:07:07,375 --> 00:07:10,950
PUPPP seldom recurs in subsequent pregnancies.

60
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:20,238
Its cause is unknown, but an autoimmune basis is not implicated (Ambros-Rudolph, 2011; Lawley, 1979).

61
00:07:20,238 --> 00:07:22,850
Figure 65-2.

62
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:32,800
Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP) shows small papules on the buttock and proximal thigh and within abdominal striae.

63
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:36,000
PUPPP mimics several other skin eruptions.

64
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:50,188
Some include contact dermatitis, drug eruption, viral exanthem, insect bites, scabies infestation, pityriasis rosea, and the other pregnancy-specific dermatoses (Brandão, 2017).

65
00:07:50,188 --> 00:07:55,500
Specifically, it may appear similar to early PG that has not yet blistered.

66
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:03,613
In unclear cases, skin biopsy and negative serum BP180 antibody levels help to differentiate the two.

67
00:08:03,613 --> 00:08:11,288
Pruritus will usually respond to treatment with oral antihistamines, skin emollients, and topical corticosteroids .

68
00:08:11,288 --> 00:08:16,825
A few women will need short-course systemic corticosteroids to relieve severe itching .

69
00:08:16,825 --> 00:08:20,175
PUPPP carries no adverse pregnancy effects.

70
00:08:20,175 --> 00:08:25,288
It usually resolves within several days following delivery and does not scar.

71
00:08:25,288 --> 00:08:31,950
In 15 to 20 percent of women, however, symptoms persist for 2 to 4 weeks postpartum .

72
00:08:31,950 --> 00:08:35,263
# Atopic Eruption of Pregnancy.

73
00:08:35,263 --> 00:08:46,075
This umbrella term encompasses three conditions previously considered separately: eczema in pregnancy, prurigo of pregnancy, and pruritic folliculitis of pregnancy .

74
00:08:46,075 --> 00:08:53,513
Two thirds of women with AEP have widespread eczematous changes, whereas the other third have papular lesions .

75
00:08:53,513 --> 00:08:59,463
Diagnosis is greatly aided by a history of prior atopy and by rash characteristics.

76
00:08:59,463 --> 00:09:03,363
As a group, these three pose no risk to the fetus .

77
00:09:03,363 --> 00:09:08,675
Eczema in pregnancy appears as traditional eczema but with a pregnancy onset.

78
00:09:08,675 --> 00:09:20,338
It is the most common pregnancy-specific dermatosis, and affected skin shows dry, thickened, scaly, red patches involving extremity flexures, nipples, neck, and face .

79
00:09:20,338 --> 00:09:34,500
In contrast, prurigo of pregnancy, also known as prurigo gestationis, is characterized by 5- to 10mm, itchy, erythematous papules or nodules commonly found on the extensor surfaces and trunk.

80
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:44,538
Last, pruritic folliculitis of pregnancy is rare and notable for small, erythematous follicular papules and sterile pustules predominantly on the trunk.

81
00:09:44,538 --> 00:09:50,463
Of AEP cases, 75 percent develop in the first or second trimesters .

82
00:09:50,463 --> 00:09:56,400
All lesions usually resolve with delivery but may persist for up to 3 months postpartum.

83
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:00,688
Recurrence with subsequent pregnancies is variable but common.

84
00:10:00,688 --> 00:10:03,475
Diagnosis is one of exclusion.

85
00:10:03,475 --> 00:10:10,613
Biopsy findings and serology results specific for PG are negative (p. 1155).

86
00:10:10,613 --> 00:10:15,475
Serum bile acid levels help exclude cholestasis of pregnancy.

87
00:10:15,475 --> 00:10:20,800
Many women with eczema of pregnancy have elevated serum immunoglobulin E

88
00:10:20,956 --> 00:10:23,043
IgE) levels .

89
00:10:22,993 --> 00:10:36,731
For all three AEP manifestations, skin lesions and pruritus are usually controlled with chronic emollient use, as-needed oral antihistamines, and low- or moderate-potency topical corticosteroid preparations .

90
00:10:36,731 --> 00:10:42,831
With the latter, twice-daily application is continued for several weeks to achieve improvement.

91
00:10:42,831 --> 00:10:49,856
For maintenance, moisturizer alone or once- or twiceweekly topical corticosteroid use is reasonable.

92
00:10:49,856 --> 00:10:56,868
For severe eczema, secondline agents include short-course ultrapotent topical corticosteroids.

93
00:10:56,868 --> 00:11:10,481
In some cases, topical tacrolimus, narrow-band ultraviolet B therapy, an oral corticosteroid, or cyclosporine is required (Eichenfield, 2014; Vestergaard, 2019).

94
00:11:10,481 --> 00:11:13,268
Other Dermatological Conditions.

95
00:11:13,268 --> 00:11:15,868
# Acne Vulgaris.

96
00:11:15,868 --> 00:11:22,568
This is a common chronic dermatosis that may worsen, improve, or remain stable during pregnancy.

97
00:11:22,568 --> 00:11:34,993
In symptomatic gravidas, either 2.5- to 5-percent benzoyl peroxide or 15- to 20-percent azelaic acid is a suitable topical, antiinflammatory, comedolytic agent .

98
00:11:34,993 --> 00:11:44,968
If needed, 5-percent benzoyl peroxide can instead be coupled with either topical erythromycin or clindamycin, and combined solutions are available.

99
00:11:44,968 --> 00:11:52,618
In these combinations, benzoyl peroxide minimizes development of Propionibacterium acnes drug resistance .

100
00:11:52,618 --> 00:11:56,706
Topical retinoids include tretinoin and adapalene.

101
00:11:56,706 --> 00:12:06,443
Large studies have not found higher rates of major birth defects with their topical use (Jick, 1993; Shapiro, 1997).

102
00:12:06,443 --> 00:12:16,456
However, these are probably best avoided during pregnancy, especially the first trimester (Kaplan, 2015; Panchaud, 2012).

103
00:12:16,456 --> 00:12:20,506
The topical retinoid tazarotene is contraindicated.

104
00:12:20,506 --> 00:12:31,343
For more severe cases, oral antibiotics that include erythromycin, azithromycin, cephalexin, or amoxicillin may be coupled with benzoyl peroxide.

105
00:12:31,343 --> 00:12:39,568
Systemic antibiotics are ideally delayed until the second trimester, and therapy duration limited to 4 to 6 weeks .

106
00:12:39,568 --> 00:12:44,156
Refractory cases often merit dermatological consultation.

107
00:12:44,156 --> 00:12:51,418
Also, for severe cases, a short course of oral corticosteroid or intralesional injection is an option.

108
00:12:51,418 --> 00:12:58,343
Of these, oral agents are delayed until after the first trimester due to the small risk for facial clefts .

109
00:12:58,343 --> 00:13:02,893
# Psoriasis and Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy.

110
00:13:02,893 --> 00:13:10,456
Psoriasis is a common dermatosis with a variable course during pregnancy, however, postpartum flares are common .

111
00:13:10,456 --> 00:13:15,093
The National Psoriasis Foundation offers pregnancy guidelines .

112
00:13:15,093 --> 00:13:22,081
Emollients alone are given initially, and low- or moderate-potency topical corticosteroids can be added.

113
00:13:22,081 --> 00:13:31,281
In resistant cases, restrained use of highpotency or ultrapotent topical corticosteroids appears safe in the second and third trimesters.

114
00:13:31,281 --> 00:13:35,318
Ultraviolet B phototherapy is a second-line option.

115
00:13:35,318 --> 00:13:48,993
Last, cyclosporine, oral corticosteroids, or tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α) antagonists that include adalimumab, etanercept, and infliximab are third-tier agents for pregnancy.

116
00:13:48,993 --> 00:13:55,281
Overall, psoriasis itself does not pose a higher risk of adverse pregnancy outcomes .

117
00:13:55,281 --> 00:14:07,156
With severe disease, a small increased risk of low birthweight, gestational diabetes, and preeclampsia was found by some (Bröms, 2018; Yang, 2011).

118
00:14:07,156 --> 00:14:11,481
Psoriasis is most commonly of the chronic plaque variety.

119
00:14:11,481 --> 00:14:19,381
In contrast, with pustular psoriasis of pregnancy, severe systemic symptoms may develop and can mimic sepsis.

120
00:14:19,381 --> 00:14:31,406
Formerly called impetigo herpetiformis, this rare pustular form has an erythematous background and pruritic plaques ringed by sterile pustules that enlarge, coalesce, and then crust .

121
00:14:31,406 --> 00:14:38,706
Plaques initially involve intertriginous areas but may spread to the torso, extremities, and oral mucosa.

122
00:14:38,706 --> 00:14:49,043
Extensive lesions can lead to massive fluid loss with hypovolemia, placental insufficiency, and fetal compromise and may also become secondarily infected.

123
00:14:49,043 --> 00:14:51,806
Figure 65-3.

124
00:14:51,806 --> 00:15:02,418
Generalized pustular psoriasis of pregnancy displays erythematous, sometimes pruritic plaques ringed by sterile pustules that enlarge and then form a scaling crust.

125
00:15:02,418 --> 00:15:06,156
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Paul Slocum.

126
00:15:06,156 --> 00:15:14,443
Testing reveals leukocytosis, elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, hypocalcemia, and hypoalbuminemia .

127
00:15:14,443 --> 00:15:20,956
Blood cultures and skin viral/bacterial cultures to exclude infection and a diagnostic

128
00:15:21,124 --> 00:15:23,799
skin biopsy are obtained.

129
00:15:23,749 --> 00:15:30,111
Classically, the former are sterile, and the latter reveals epidermal pustules of neutrophils.

130
00:15:30,111 --> 00:15:49,299
First-line treatment is with oral prednisone (15–80 mg/d), cyclosporine (2–3 mg/kg/d), infliximab (Remicade), topical corticosteroids, or topical calcipotriene (Robinson, 2012; Trivedi, 2018).

131
00:15:49,299 --> 00:15:55,061
Selection of a systemic or topical agent and its dose is directed by disease severity.

132
00:15:55,061 --> 00:16:03,774
As needed, fluid resuscitation, electrolyte correction, and intravenous antibiotics for secondary infections are adjuncts .

133
00:16:03,774 --> 00:16:11,924
Pustular psoriasis of pregnancy typically resolves quickly postpartum, and thus early delivery can be considered individually.

134
00:16:11,924 --> 00:16:18,311
Recurrences are reported in subsequent pregnancies and with menses or oral contraceptive use .

135
00:16:18,311 --> 00:16:21,024
# Erythema Nodosum.

136
00:16:21,024 --> 00:16:29,011
This skin condition reflects subcutaneous fat inflammation and is associated with many disorders, including pregnancy.

137
00:16:29,011 --> 00:16:41,136
Other triggers are infections, sarcoidosis, drugs, Behçet syndrome, inflammatory bowel disease, or malignancy (Mert, 2007; Papagrigoraki, 2010).

138
00:16:41,136 --> 00:16:49,924
Characteristically, tender, red, warm, 1- to 6-cm nodules and plaques develop rapidly on extensor surfaces.

139
00:16:49,924 --> 00:16:57,411
After a few days, lesions flatten, and color evolves like a bruise— from dark red and purple to yellow green.

140
00:16:57,411 --> 00:17:00,874
Constitutional symptoms also may be present.

141
00:17:00,874 --> 00:17:05,874
Initial evaluation and treatment focuses on the underlying etiology.

142
00:17:05,874 --> 00:17:12,924
Symptoms spontaneously resolve in 1 to 6 weeks without scarring but may leave residual hyperpigmentation .

143
00:17:12,924 --> 00:17:15,849
# Pyogenic Granuloma.

144
00:17:15,849 --> 00:17:19,236
This lesion is frequently seen in pregnancy .

145
00:17:19,236 --> 00:17:25,386
Poorly named, pyogenic granuloma is actually a lobular capillary hemangioma.

146
00:17:25,386 --> 00:17:36,161
Pregnancy-associated elevations in angiogenic factors that include vascular endothelial growth factor are implicated (Yuan, 2000, 2004).

147
00:17:36,161 --> 00:17:45,024
It commonly forms on the gingiva, other oral/nasal surfaces, or hand in response to lowgrade local irritation or trauma .

148
00:17:45,024 --> 00:17:48,974
Lesions grow quickly and bleed with minimal provocation.

149
00:17:48,974 --> 00:17:57,186
Active bleeding can be controlled with pressure and application of a silver nitrate stick or Monsel (ferric subsulfate) paste.

150
00:17:57,186 --> 00:18:02,886
These growths often resolve within months postpartum and thus may be observed antepartum.

151
00:18:02,886 --> 00:18:11,349
But, with a symptomatic antepartum growth, a persistent postpartum lesion, or an unclear diagnosis, excision is preferred.

152
00:18:11,349 --> 00:18:19,661
An option is scalpel excision plus suturing, electrosurgical curettage, laser photocoagulation, or cryotherapy.

153
00:18:19,661 --> 00:18:23,074
Oral lesions are best referred to specialists.

154
00:18:23,074 --> 00:18:25,811
Figure 65-4.

155
00:18:25,811 --> 00:18:33,149
Pyogenic granuloma is characterized grossly by a lobulated red growth on a pedunculated or sessile base.

156
00:18:33,149 --> 00:18:37,486
With minimal trauma, these vascular lesions bleed easily.

157
00:18:37,486 --> 00:18:41,111
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Sarah White.

158
00:18:41,111 --> 00:18:43,999
# Miscellaneous Conditions.

159
00:18:43,999 --> 00:18:48,899
Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic inflammatory skin disease.

160
00:18:48,899 --> 00:18:57,324
Keratin plugging of hair follicles and recurrent inflammation lead to painful nodules and abscesses that rupture to create sinus tracts.

161
00:18:57,324 --> 00:19:07,761
Hurley stages I–III range from transient, widely spaced inflammatory papules to diffuse involvement with multiple deep sinus tracts between skin abscesses .

162
00:19:07,761 --> 00:19:16,624
For most, symptoms during pregnancy are unchanged or improved (Kromann, 2014; Vossen, 2017).

163
00:19:16,624 --> 00:19:22,499
Treatment aims to decrease cutaneous inflammation and provide antibacterial effects.

164
00:19:22,499 --> 00:19:30,174
For active stage I/II disease, 1-percent clindamycin is topically applied twice daily for 12 weeks .

165
00:19:30,174 --> 00:19:37,774
As alternatives, 0.75-percent metronidazole or 2-percent erythromycin gel are suitable .

166
00:19:37,774 --> 00:19:49,149
For stage II/III disease, a 300-mg clindamycin plus a 300-mg rifampin dose orally twice daily for 10 weeks has evidence-based support .

167
00:19:49,149 --> 00:20:03,924
Adalimumab (Humira) is an FDA-approved biological agent that targets TNF-α and offers a second-line option for advanced-stage disease in pregnancy (Jemec, 2019; Perng, 2017).

168
00:20:03,924 --> 00:20:17,524
Neurofibromatosis lesions are typified by benign cutaneous neurofibromas, café-au-lait spots, axillary and inguinal freckling, benign iris nodules (Lisch nodules), and optic nerve gliomas.

169
00:20:17,524 --> 00:20:26,311
Neurofibromas may grow in size and number during pregnancy (Cesaretti, 2013; Dugoff, 1996).

170
00:20:26,311 --> 00:20:32,474
With neurofibromatosis type 1, higher rates of preeclampsia and preterm birth

171
00:20:32,644 --> 00:20:38,969
complicate pregnancy (Leppävirta, 2017; Terry, 2013).

172
00:20:38,919 --> 00:20:43,806
With type 2, some evidence suggests a risk for preeclampsia .

173
00:20:43,806 --> 00:20:48,019
Prenatal genetic diagnosis is available for both types .

174
00:20:48,019 --> 00:20:58,231
Rosacea fulminans, also known as pyoderma faciale, is rare and typified by facial pustules and coalescing draining sinuses but without comedones.

175
00:20:58,231 --> 00:21:03,181
Its marked presentation usually merits dermatological consultation.

176
00:21:03,181 --> 00:21:10,019
Topical or oral antibiotics are primary treatment, and topical corticosteroids may be added.

177
00:21:10,019 --> 00:21:22,594
Less often, short-course oral corticosteroids or focal surgical drainage is a needed adjunct (Angileri, 2021; Fuentelsaz, 2011; Jarrett, 2010).

178
00:21:22,594 --> 00:21:33,656
Other skin conditions discussed elsewhere in this book are hirsutism and melanoma , cutaneous lupus , hyperpigmentation , and skin lesions seen with infections (Chaps.

179
00:21:33,656 --> 00:21:37,806
67 and 68, p. 1182).

180
00:21:37,806 --> 00:21:40,231
Dermatological Treatment.

181
00:21:40,231 --> 00:21:47,919
Local skin care, oral antihistamines, and topical corticosteroids are commonly used for many dermatoses.

182
00:21:47,919 --> 00:21:51,431
Oral antihistamines are given for pruritus.

183
00:21:51,431 --> 00:22:05,006
Suitable options include first-generation agents such as diphenhydramine (Benadryl), 25 to 50 mg every 6 hours, or chlorpheniramine (Chlor-Trimeton), 4 mg every 6 hours.

184
00:22:05,006 --> 00:22:17,869
Second-generation agents—loratadine (Claritin) 10 mg daily or cetirizine (Zyrtec) 5 or 10 mg daily—may produce less sedation and are also safe for pregnancy.

185
00:22:17,869 --> 00:22:25,806
Numerous topical corticosteroid preparations are available, and in the United States, these are categorized by potency .

186
00:22:25,806 --> 00:22:32,206
For initial treatment of dermatological disorders, low- or moderate-potency agents are preferred.

187
00:22:32,206 --> 00:22:39,769
Low-potency agents include 1-percent hydrocortisone and 0.05percent desonide (DesOwen).

188
00:22:39,769 --> 00:22:49,719
Moderate-potency drugs are 0.1-percent triamcinolone acetonide (Aristocort) and 0.1-percent mometasone furoate (Elocon).

189
00:22:49,719 --> 00:22:56,856
High-potency medications include 0.05-percent betamethasone dipropionate (Diprolene).

190
00:22:56,856 --> 00:23:09,531
Ultrapotent agents, such as 0.05-percent clobetasol propionate (Temovate), are best reserved for refractory disorders and used for only 2 to 4 weeks on small surface areas.

191
00:23:09,531 --> 00:23:12,156
Table 65-2.

192
00:23:12,156 --> 00:23:30,931
Commonly Used Topical Corticosteroids in Pregnancy Mild and moderate strengths are not associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, whereas high-potency and ultrapotent agents pose a small risk for fetal-growth restriction with large cumulative doses (Chi, 2013, 2015).

193
00:23:30,931 --> 00:23:35,694
Even then, this risk is less than that with oral corticosteroids.

194
00:23:35,694 --> 00:23:52,206
Importantly, with any topical agent, factors that raise systemic absorption include a large surface area treated, compromised epidermal barrier, occlusive dressing, prolonged treatment duration, and coadministration of other topical agents that enhance absorption.

195
00:23:52,206 --> 00:24:02,956
For use in pregnancy and lactation, Murase (2014) and Butler (2014) have compiled tables and evidence-based descriptions of most.

196
00:24:02,956 --> 00:24:07,931
Table 65-3 reflects more commonly encountered agents.

197
00:24:07,931 --> 00:24:19,431
Notable therapeutic agents to avoid during pregnancy include methotrexate, psoralen plus ultraviolet A, mycophenolate mofetil, podophyllin, and systemic retinoids.

198
00:24:19,431 --> 00:24:23,031
These teratogens are discussed in Chapter 8.

199
00:24:23,031 --> 00:24:33,081
Bacterial infections are a potential secondary complication of skin disorders and are treated promptly with oral antimicrobial agents with gram-positive coverage.

200
00:24:33,081 --> 00:24:35,781
Table 65-3.

201
00:24:35,781 --> 00:24:40,306
